WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.080
If you're sensitive to flashing images

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.560
I'd give this entire video a
miss because it's full of them.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.820
Hello there, I am Gav,

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.380
in this video we'll be
learning how a 3D TV works,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.060
using the help of slow motion as usual.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.560
Specifically the kind that use these

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.100
active shutter 3D glasses.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.550
Should probably start saying
this is how a 3D TV worked,

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.060
because they're now relics of the past.

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.620
This video is sponsored by KiwiCo,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.010
I'll tell you more about them at the end.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.910
Before we look at the TV specifically

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.090
let's just go over the basics
of what is actually going on

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.660
when you watch a 3D movie.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.970
When watching content the illusion of 3D

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:41.520
is achieved by showing two
different perspectives,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.210
one to each eye.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.970
This process is usually
started in the camera

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:50.090
either by using a special
3D camera with two lenses,

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:54.130
or by using two cameras side
by side to mimic the positions

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:55.860
of two human eyeballs.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.850
For our VR180 series on Oculus

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:59.830
for our regular speed footage,

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.230
we used a camera with two lenses,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.880
and then for the slow-mo
we just use two Phantoms,

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:05.740
side-by-side.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.010
Now the Phantoms are
actually physically too big

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.170
to get that close together

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.680
especially once the lenses were on.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.210
So we used a special mirroring,

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:14.880
one camera looking through a mirror

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.130
and one looking off the
reflection of the mirror.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.080
And this allowed the
Phantoms to effectively

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.500
phase through each other,

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.030
giving the correct interocular distance.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.670
From experience, I can
tell you that this method

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:29.510
introduces a lot of extra
costs and a lot of extra time

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.480
needed to complete the shoot.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.190
I also wouldn't recommend
handheld shots with this setup.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.970
So now we have our two
different perspectives,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:38.260
left and right eye.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.400
The next challenge is only showing

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.110
the correct perspective to each eye.

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.130
In a VR headset, it's
actually quite simple.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:47.780
You just have each eye
look at a different screen,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.640
or look through a different lens

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.850
looking at different
areas on the same screen.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.260
I won't go into too much
detail about the other methods,

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.710
there's great videos online already.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.510
But in a movie theater you would typically

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.300
project both perspectives using
differently polarized light

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.860
and then un-powered glasses
would filter those correctly

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:04.740
into each eye.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.910
You can also use Anaglyph,

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:09.080
which typically uses
red and blue filtering

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.880
to show each perspective to each eye.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.430
We actually use this out in the field

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:15.540
just to make sure our
footage was looking correct

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.820
before we could properly
get it into post-production.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.300
But it is a pretty ugly
way to view 3D content

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.660
because the colors are
just all over the place.

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:24.090
That brings us onto this TV.

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.580
It's actually a plasma 3D TV,

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.660
which I bought secondhand off Craigslist.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:29.930
It's not in great condition,

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:31.390
there's all kinds of mess happening

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.440
on the left side of the screen

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.890
and the stand is taped
on with a bit of gaffer,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.990
but it should perform
just fine for this demo.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:40.240
The reason I went for plasma
is that we can actually

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.790
get a bit of a double whammy here.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.490
I've shown how CRT screens
work, OLED screens work,

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.690
and how regular LED LCD screens work.

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.320
Never had a plasma TV before

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.420
and the way they work
is slightly different.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.980
Like an OLED TV, each
pixel is self-emissive.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.310
They don't need a backlight,

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:01.310
but they refresh the screen
in multiple pulses per frame.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.540
The entire screen can't
be lit at full brightness.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.150
So it ends up doing very
short duration pulses

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.810
of different information in the frame

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:12.990
Persistence of vision in your brain

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.880
causes you just to see the complete image.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.260
This is known as the sub-field drive

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.980
which often occurs up
to 10 times per frame.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.080
And the pulse is seen to last
slightly different lengths

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.490
depending on where they
are in building the frame.

00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:30.760
Here I've stacked the results
of each individual pulse.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.800
Without even realizing it,

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.880
your brain will have stitched
all of these together

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.640
creating what looks like just
a normal frame of a movie.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.960
Based on what we're
seeing here this plasma TV

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:43.220
probably would have been
advertised as a 480 Hertz display,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.480
but that doesn't mean it's capable

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.070
of displaying 480 frames per second,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.630
it just means that it's a 60 Hertz display

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.920
with eight pulses per frame.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.570
So the way this differs
from an OLED or an LCD TV

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.300
is that in slow motion,
you can't actually see

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.210
the entire frame at once.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.210
So now if we switch to 3D mode,

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:03.210
to the human eye without glasses on,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:04.810
it looks like both perspectives

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.760
are just blurred over each other.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.400
However, if I turn up my
shutter speed on this camera,

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.210
removing a lot of the motion blur,

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.130
you can see that they're
not just stationary

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:15.760
sat over each other,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.640
it's flicking back and forth very quickly.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.330
So now we know that once
the TV has completed

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:24.710
building out one frame, it
then switches perspective

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.500
to the other eye and builds the same frame

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.070
from that perspective.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.730
This is where the active
shutter glasses come in,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.270
and their job is to
make sure your left eye

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.840
only sees the left frame

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.840
and your right eye only
sees the right frame.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.950
And it does it by doing this.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:45.880
Using a battery inside to
power liquid crystal technology

00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:49.110
inside each lens, very
similar to what you would find

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.500
in an LCD TV.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.030
It switches back and forth

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.230
blocking the vision from each eye.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.330
This will happen 60 times
per second, per eye.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.970
That's 120 switches per second,

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.000
even on films that are only playing back

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.573
at 24 frames a second.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.110
Looking through the
glasses here in 3D mode

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:18.080
slowed down to 20,000 frames per second,

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.000
we can see that the TV now
only has the capability

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.400
to pulse three times per
eye for a total of six.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.250
And I assume the remaining two pulses

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.683
are used to switch between
left and right eye.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:31.723
Well there we have it.

00:05:31.723 --> 00:05:34.610
I honestly think that's such
an interesting way to show

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.090
a different image to each eye

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.110
because the concept is so simple.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:41.220
It's really just using
technology to perform

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:42.620
advanced winking.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.010
It honestly makes me a little bit sad

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.980
that the technology has
been abandoned for new TVs

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.380
because you can get such big
TVs now for so much cheaper

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.960
than they were back when this
technology was being used.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:57.640
And this makes more sense to
me to have on a much screen,

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.030
otherwise it kind of looks
like you're watching a film

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.800
in a box that's like a foot deep,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.930
it doesn't make as much sense
to me on a small screen.

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:05.763
Yeah, I never had one,

00:06:05.763 --> 00:06:07.140
I guess I'm a part of
the reason they went away

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.830
because I never bought a 3D TV.

